We conducted an experiment to explore whether humans could learn to change the relative activation of their gastrocnemius and soleus muscles during walking when given visual biofeedback. During a single session, 10 healthy adults performed five six-minute walking trials on an instrumented treadmill: a baseline trial, three feedback trials, and a retention trial (Fig. 2b). During all feedback trials, a real-time bar plot instructed participants to reduce their gastrocnemius-to-soleus activation ratio (Eq. 3):3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
